---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';

const entry = {
  "id": "harvard_vi4_244",
  "series": "Band VI 4 (1677 - 1690)",
  "seq": 244,
  "text": "[[942.1]] <b>201.  De termino, praedicato, relatione [August 1688 bis Oktober 1690 (?)]</b>\n [[942.2]] <tab/>[August 1688 bis Oktober 1690 (?)] </p>\n<p> [[942.16]] Terminus omnis propositionem ingreditur in recto vel in obliquo. Qui in recto est\n[[942.17]] primarius, et aliis suppressis sensum facit; qui in obliquo ingreditur est dependens.\n[[942.18]] Terminus qui in recto ingreditur est subjectum vel praedicatum vel certe horum pars\n[[942.19]] primaria. </p>\n<p> [[942.20]] Etsi duo termini propositionem non in recto sed obliquo ingrediantur, tamen fieri\n[[942.21]] potest ut in recto id est homogenee praedicentur de se invicem, hoc est, ut uno existente\n[[942.22]] recto etiam alter sit in recto; v.g. si dicatur: <i>Ego novi hominem doctum, homo</i> et <i>doctus</i> se\n[[942.23]] afficiunt in recto. </p>\n<p> [[942.24]] Praedicatur terminus de termino, vel homogenee seu in recto, vel heterogenee seu in\n[[942.25]] obliquo. Praedicatum in recto est vel latius vel aequale vel angustius. Vel si mavis est\n[[942.26]] universale vel particulare; et universale vel latius vel aequale. Rursus est vel proprium vel\n[[942.27]] commune, et proprium vel angustius vel aequale. Coincidunt inter se: universale aequale\n[[942.28]] et proprium aequale, sed non omne commune est universale; sic beatum esse commune\n[[942.29]] est homini cum angelis, sed neutri universale, neque enim omnes homines aut omnes\n[[942.30]] angeli sunt beati; neque omne proprium est particulare; nam risus est homini proprius et\n[[943.1]] omni homini competit. Datur igitur proprium universale ut risus, omne proprium universale\n[[943.2]] est aequale. Non datur proprium latius, nec universale angustius. </p>\n<p> [[943.3]] Praedicatum est vel substantivum vel adjectivum. Praedicatum substantivum latius\n[[943.4]] est genus; praedicatum substantivum angustius est species vel individuum; praedicatum\n[[943.5]] substantivum aequale est synonymum. Inter praedicatum substantivum et adjectivum\n[[943.6]] videtur hoc interesse, quod qui dicit <i>Petrus est homo</i>, dicit <i>Petrus est substantia rationalis</i>,\n[[943.7]] qui vero dicit <i>Petrus est rationalis</i> non meminit substantiae, etsi eadem insit. Itaque\n[[943.8]] nullum ego usum magnopere substantivorum concretorum prae adjectivis video. Illud\n[[943.9]] tamen videtur quodammodo subesse: <i>substantia rationalis, mortalis</i>, et <i>substantia risiva</i>,\n[[943.10]] differre videntur a termino, <i>homo</i>, quod terminus <i>homo</i> formaliciter et immediate dicere\n[[943.11]] intelligitur omnia quae de omni eo, de quo <i>homo</i> praedicatur vere praedicari possunt; <Fn>NB. NB.</Fn>\n[[943.12]] nempe cum <i>hominem</i> dico, intelligo substantiam illam quae rationalis, mortalis est, quae\n[[943.13]] risiva est, quae caeteris orbis terrarum animalibus dominatur, aliaque omnia quae de ea\n[[943.14]] praedicari possunt. Cum vero <i>substantiam rationalem</i> dico, caetera quidem dico per\n[[943.15]] consequentiam, sed non immediate et collective. Nescio tamen, an magnus hujus rei sit\n[[943.16]] usus. </p>\n<p> [[943.17]] Porro praedicatum est vel simplex vel compositum. Et compositum vel in recto vel\n[[943.18]] in obliquo. In recto componi potest copulative vel disjunctive. Ex. g. copulative, <i>Omne\n[[943.19]] quadratum est rectangulum aequilaterum</i>, seu <i>rectangulum et aequilaterum.</i> Disjunctive,\n[[943.20]] ut <i>Omnis Sectio Conica est Parabola, Hyperbola vel Ellipsis.</i> Praedicatum in recto\n[[943.21]] imperfectum est, cum aliquid (in obliquo) adjici debet ad sensum supplendum, v.g. <i>Deus\n[[943.22]] produxit seu fuit</i> [<i>producens</i>]. Expectatur quid produxerit, v.g. coelum et terram. </p>\n<p> [[943.23]] Quod nondum praestitum a quoquam memini est investigatio obliquitatum. Et discernitur\n[[943.24]] per casus et praepositiones, daturque adeo obliquitas obliquitatis. </p>\n<p> [[943.25]] Videntur plerunque substantiva regere genitivum, <i>sermo hominis</i>, non dicimus\n[[943.26]] <i>sermo homini</i>, sed <i>sermo factus homini.</i> </p>\n<p> [[944.1]] <b>Relationes</b> sunt vel comparationis vel connexionis. <b>Relatio comparationis</b>\n[[944.2]] ex eo nascitur inter <i>A</i> et <i>B</i>, quod <i>A</i> reperitur in aliqua propositione, et <i>B</i> in alia propositione,\n[[944.3]] <b>Relatio connexionis</b> ex eo quod tam <i>A</i> et <i>B</i> sunt in una eademque propositione\n[[944.4]] (quae in relationem comparationis resolvi non potest). Nam alioqui etiam relatio\n[[944.5]] comparationis foret relatio connexionis, nam formari potest una propositio comprehendens\n[[944.6]] <i>A</i> et <i>B</i>, nempe <i>A est similis B.</i> Sed ea resolvitur tandem in duas quarum una\n[[944.7]] singulatim agit de <i>B</i>, altera sigillatim de <i>A</i>, verb. gr. <i>A est ruber</i> et <i>B est ruber</i>, et ideo <i>A</i>\n[[944.8]] est similis (quoad hoc) ipsi <i>B.</i> Per <i>A</i> autem et <i>B</i>, intelliguntur res seu individua non\n[[944.9]] termini. Sed quid de his dicemus: <i>A</i> existit hodie, et <i>B</i> etiam existit hodie, seu <i>A</i> et <i>B</i>\n[[944.10]] existunt simul? An erit haec relatio comparationis, an connexionis. Idem est de coexistentia\n[[944.11]] in eodem loco. </p>\n<p> [[944.12]] Videntur relationes inter duas res vel esse solum rationales vel reales, seu esse\n[[944.13]] Essentiae vel Existentiae. Reales sunt vel Positionis (temporis nempe et situs), vel\n[[944.14]] Influxus; cum per unum Mutatio aliqua fit vel impeditur in aliquo. Ut cum dico <i>Homo est\n[[944.15]] mortalis</i>, et <i>animal est mortale</i>, est relatio rationis, et haec dat quod vulgo vocant\n[[944.16]] denominationem extrinsecam, quod et dici potest de Relatione positionis. Itaque si dicam\n[[944.17]] <i>Petrus distat a me 100 leucis</i> est denominatio extrinseca, et si ego movear quiescente\n[[944.18]] Petro, mutatur quidem distantia Petri a me, sed sine mutatione Petri, nisi quae fit in\n[[944.19]] virtute connexionis rerum universalis. Sed relatio influxus denominationes intrinsecas\n[[944.20]] parit, quanquam in praeteritis etiam extrinsecis serviat. Sic si pater mihi oriatur verum\n[[944.21]] quidem est relationem inter me et patrem esse influxus; sed nunc cum cesset influxus,\n[[944.22]] <i>amissio patris</i> denominatio (per se) extrinseca est. </p>\n<p> [[944.23]] Relationes fundantur in Essentia vel Existentia. Quae in Existentia sunt Positionis\n[[944.24]] vel Influxus. Rursus [<i>bricht ab</i>] </p>\n",
  "number": "201.",
  "title": "De termino, praedicato, relatione",
  "date": "[August 1688 bis Oktober 1690 (?)]"
};
---

<Layout title={`${entry.title || entry.id} - Leibniz Writings`}>
  <article data-pagefind-body>
    <header class="entry-header">
      <h1 data-pagefind-meta="title">{entry.title || entry.id}</h1>

      <div class="entry-meta">
        {entry.date && (
          <div class="meta-item">
            <strong>Date:</strong>
            <span data-pagefind-meta="date">{entry.date}</span>
          </div>
        )}

        <div class="meta-item">
          <strong>Series:</strong>
          <span data-pagefind-meta="series">{entry.series}</span>
        </div>

        {entry.number && (
          <div class="meta-item">
            <strong>Number:</strong>
            <span data-pagefind-meta="number">{entry.number}</span>
          </div>
        )}

        <div class="meta-item">
          <strong>Sequence:</strong>
          <span>{entry.seq}</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </header>

    <!-- View Toggle -->
    <div class="view-controls">
      <button id="translation-view-btn" class="view-btn active">Translation View</button>
      <button id="scholarly-view-btn" class="view-btn">Scholarly View</button>
    </div>

    <div class="entry-content" data-pagefind-meta="excerpt:201. De termino, praedicato, relatione [August 1688 bis Oktober 1690 (?)] [August 1688 bis Oktober 1690 (?)] Terminus omnis propositionem ingreditur in recto vel in obliquo. Qui in recto est primarius, et aliis suppressis sensum facit; qui in obliquo ingreditur est dependens. Terminus qui in recto i...">

      <!-- Translation-friendly continuous text -->
      <div id="translation-view" class="text-view active">
        <div class="translation-notice">
          <p><strong>📖 Translation View:</strong> Continuous text optimized for browser translation tools.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="continuous-text" translate="yes" lang="unknown" id="continuous-text">
          <!-- Will be populated by JavaScript -->
        </div>
        <div class="line-numbers-reference" id="line-numbers-ref">
          <!-- Will be populated by JavaScript -->
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Detailed scholarly view with line numbers -->
      <div id="scholarly-view" class="text-view">
        <div class="scholarly-notice">
          <p><strong>📚 Scholarly View:</strong> Original text with line number annotations preserved.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="annotated-text" id="annotated-text">
          {entry.text.split('\n').map(paragraph =>
            paragraph.trim() && <p set:html={paragraph} />
          )}
        </div>
      </div>

    </div>
  </article>

  <nav class="entry-navigation">
    <a href="/" class="back-link">← Back to Search</a>
  </nav>
</Layout>

<style>
  .entry-header {
    margin-bottom: 30px;
    padding-bottom: 20px;
    border-bottom: 2px solid var(--border);
  }

  .entry-header h1 {
    color: var(--primary);
    font-size: 2rem;
    margin-bottom: 20px;
    line-height: 1.3;
  }

  .entry-meta {
    display: grid;
    gap: 8px;
    font-size: 0.95rem;
  }

  .meta-item {
    display: flex;
    align-items: flex-start;
    gap: 8px;
  }

  .meta-item strong {
    color: var(--text);
    min-width: 80px;
  }

  .meta-item span {
    color: var(--text-secondary);
    flex: 1;
  }

  .entry-content {
    margin-bottom: 40px;
  }

  .text-content {
    background: var(--surface);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 8px;
    padding: 30px;
    line-height: 1.7;
  }

  .text-content :global(p) {
    margin-bottom: 16px;
  }

  .text-content :global(p:last-child) {
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }

  .text-content :global(b) {
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--primary);
  }

  .entry-navigation {
    border-top: 1px solid var(--border);
    padding-top: 20px;
  }

  .back-link {
    color: var(--primary);
    text-decoration: none;
    font-weight: 500;
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 8px;
    transition: color 0.2s;
  }

  .back-link:hover {
    color: var(--primary-dark);
    text-decoration: underline;
  }

  /* View Toggle Controls */
  .view-controls {
    display: flex;
    gap: 8px;
    margin-bottom: 20px;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);
    padding-bottom: 20px;
  }

  .view-btn {
    background: transparent;
    border: 2px solid var(--border);
    padding: 8px 16px;
    border-radius: 6px;
    cursor: pointer;
    font-size: 14px;
    font-weight: 500;
    color: var(--text-secondary);
    transition: all 0.2s;
  }

  .view-btn:hover {
    border-color: var(--primary);
    color: var(--primary);
  }

  .view-btn.active {
    background: var(--primary);
    border-color: var(--primary);
    color: white;
  }

  /* Text Views */
  .text-view {
    display: none;
  }

  .text-view.active {
    display: block;
  }

  /* Translation View */
  .translation-notice, .scholarly-notice {
    background: var(--background);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 6px;
    padding: 12px;
    margin-bottom: 20px;
    font-size: 14px;
  }

  .continuous-text {
    background: var(--surface);
    border: 2px solid var(--primary);
    border-radius: 8px;
    padding: 25px;
    line-height: 1.8;
    font-size: 16px;
    color: var(--text);
    margin-bottom: 15px;
  }

  .line-numbers-reference {
    color: var(--text-secondary);
    font-size: 13px;
    padding: 10px;
    background: var(--background);
    border-radius: 4px;
  }

  /* Scholarly View */
  .annotated-text {
    background: var(--surface);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 8px;
    padding: 20px;
  }

  .text-line {
    display: flex;
    align-items: flex-start;
    margin-bottom: 8px;
    gap: 12px;
  }

  .line-refs {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 4px;
    min-width: 80px;
    flex-shrink: 0;
  }

  .line-num {
    background: var(--primary);
    color: white;
    padding: 2px 6px;
    border-radius: 4px;
    font-size: 11px;
    font-weight: 600;
    font-family: monospace;
  }

  .line-content {
    flex: 1;
    line-height: 1.6;
  }

  .line-content :global(b) {
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--primary);
  }

  .line-content :global(i) {
    font-style: italic;
    color: var(--text-secondary);
  }

  @media (min-width: 768px) {
    .entry-meta {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr);
    }
  }

  @media (max-width: 768px) {
    .view-controls {
      flex-direction: column;
    }

    .text-line {
      flex-direction: column;
      gap: 6px;
    }

    .line-refs {
      min-width: auto;
    }
  }
</style>

<script>
  // Process text for translation view
  function processTextForTranslation(text) {
    if (!text) return { translationText: '', lineNumbers: [] };

    const lines = text.split('\n');
    let translationText = '';
    const lineNumbers = [];

    lines.forEach(line => {
      const trimmedLine = line.trim();
      if (!trimmedLine) return;

      // Extract line numbers
      const lineNumberMatch = trimmedLine.match(/\[\[(\d+\.\d+)\]\]/g);
      const extractedNumbers = lineNumberMatch ?
        lineNumberMatch.map(m => m.replace(/\[|\]/g, '')) : [];

      // Remove line number markers and HTML tags for continuous text
      let contentOnly = trimmedLine
        .replace(/\[\[\d+\.\d+\]\]/g, '')
        .replace(/<\/?[^>]+(>|$)/g, '')
        .replace(/\s+/g, ' ')
        .trim();

      if (contentOnly) {
        if (translationText) translationText += ' ';
        translationText += contentOnly;
        lineNumbers.push(...extractedNumbers);
      }
    });

    return {
      translationText: translationText.trim(),
      lineNumbers: lineNumbers
    };
  }

  // View switching and text processing
  document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', () => {
    const translationBtn = document.getElementById('translation-view-btn');
    const scholarlyBtn = document.getElementById('scholarly-view-btn');
    const translationView = document.getElementById('translation-view');
    const scholarlyView = document.getElementById('scholarly-view');
    const continuousTextDiv = document.getElementById('continuous-text');
    const lineNumbersRef = document.getElementById('line-numbers-ref');

    // Process the text for translation view
    if (continuousTextDiv && lineNumbersRef && entry && entry.text) {
      const processed = processTextForTranslation(entry.text);
      continuousTextDiv.textContent = processed.translationText;

      if (processed.lineNumbers.length > 0) {
        lineNumbersRef.innerHTML = `<small><strong>Line numbers:</strong> ${processed.lineNumbers.join(', ')}</small>`;
      }
    }

    // View switching functionality
    if (translationBtn && scholarlyBtn && translationView && scholarlyView) {
      translationBtn.addEventListener('click', () => {
        translationBtn.classList.add('active');
        scholarlyBtn.classList.remove('active');
        translationView.classList.add('active');
        scholarlyView.classList.remove('active');
      });

      scholarlyBtn.addEventListener('click', () => {
        scholarlyBtn.classList.add('active');
        translationBtn.classList.remove('active');
        scholarlyView.classList.add('active');
        translationView.classList.remove('active');
      });
    }
  });
</script>